<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android Auto Backup is allowed to take, on API 30 and below.
  `data_extraction_rules.xml` is the same decision for API 31 and above; both
  are named from the manifest and both have to agree.

  One directory, and everything else by omission. Naming an `<include>` at all
  is what changes the default: from that point Android backs up *only* the
  paths listed, so the app's data directory — the unencrypted portfolio
  database in it — is out without needing to say so.

  It did say so, at first: five `<exclude>` lines, one per domain, added as
  belt and braces. They were inert, and `lintVitalRelease` refuses to ship
  them — "`.` is not in an included path" — because an exclude outside the
  included set can never apply to anything. The rules that survive a release
  build are the rules that do something.

  `files/backup/` is empty until somebody turns on Settings → Privacy →
  "Include a copy in Google backup". What it then holds is the app's own
  export rather than the database: a raw SQLite file from an older schema is a
  migration problem on restore, and the export is the one format this app
  reads from any version of itself.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="file" path="backup/" />
</full-backup-content>
